<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kostroma</h1><p class="st-meta">1246 CE – 1276 CE (approx.) · capital Kostroma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Volga appanage whose only prince, Vasily, rose to become grand prince of Vladimir.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> was a town of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a> lands. In 1246, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a>, his youngest son Vasily received Kostroma as his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-of-kostroma">Vasily of Kostroma</a> was an active prince who fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> and the Novgorodians. In 1272 he became grand prince of Vladimir with the approval of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> rather than moving to Vladimir. The principality was a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> land with little else recorded.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Vasily died in 1276 without sons, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostroma">Kostroma</a> passed back to the grand princes of Vladimir. It later became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ipatiev-monastery">Ipatiev Monastery</a> near the town later played a part in the election of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-of-russia">Michael Romanov</a> in 1613.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1246 CE – 1276 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.50%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1246 CE – 1276 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kostroma</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vasily of Kostroma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kostroma Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1261&amp;focus=principality-of-kostroma" aria-label="Principality of Kostroma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5555 224 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kostroma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5875 480L5878 523"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5878 523h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5875 480h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5875 480h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kostroma. <a href="/atlas?year=1261&amp;focus=principality-of-kostroma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
